  "Novelist, historian and poet; the finest woman novelist of the Irish revival and the author of Hurrish and Grania.",
  "Born the Hon. Emily Lawless at Lyons, County Kildare, on 17 June 1845, the daughter of the third Baron Cloncurry; she grew up in Kildare and in the Burren of County Clare. Hurrish (1886), Grania (1892), Maelcho (1894) and the poems With the Wild Geese (1902) followed. She died at Gomshall, Surrey, on 2 December 1913.",
  "Emily Lawless was the finest woman novelist of the Irish revival: Hurrish and Grania brought the west of Ireland and the Aran Islands into fiction with a realism that neither the Celtic Twilight nor the peasant play could match, and With the Wild Geese is one of the great Irish historical poem sequences.",
  "The western landscape as the determining force; the Aran and Burren detail; the heroine divided between two men and two Irelands; the historical retrospect of the Jacobite wars; the plain, unsentimental prose.",
  "Ireland, Home Rule, and the national question; the countryside, the vanishing rural world; the woman question and the limited estate; the Celtic periphery and the imperial centre; national identity and the heroic past.",
  "Emily Lawless was the finest woman novelist of the Irish revival: Hurrish and Grania brought the west of Ireland and the Aran Islands into fiction with a realism the Celtic Twilight could not match, and With the Wild Geese is one of the great Irish historical poem sequences.",
