  "Novelist, reviewer and correspondent; the author of The Half Sisters and one of the most influential literary women of the mid-Victorian period.",
  "Born Geraldine Endsor Jewsbury at Measham, Derbyshire, on 22 August 1812; she moved to Manchester and then London, where she became the reader for Bentley's and a reviewer for the Athenaeum, and the intimate friend of Jane Welsh Carlyle. Zoe (1845), The Half Sisters (1848), Marian Withers (1851) and Constance Herbert (1855) followed. She died in London on 23 September 1880.",
  "Geraldine Jewsbury wrote The Half Sisters, one of the most important English novels of the woman's vocation and a founding text of the novel of female independence: her Manchester setting, her professional heroine and her argument that a woman's work is her life made her the most radical of the pre-Braddon novelists and a model for the New Woman fiction.",
  "The double heroine divided between marriage and vocation; the Manchester commercial setting; the argument in dialogue; the reviewer's irony; the refusal of the conventional ending.",
